tter to discard a tile that's less useful. For example, W7 or W9. If I discard W7, I can aim for W8 to form W7-W8-W9. But W8 is only 2 left. If I discard W7, maybe others will take it, but if I get W8, I can form the chow. However, since W8 is less available, maybe that's riskier.\n\nAnother angle: looking at the game history, the next player has played T8 multiple times. The player across has played T7, T9, etc. So maybe T8 is in play. If I discard T6, I can aim for T8. But T8 is only 1 left. If I discard T6, maybe someone else will take T8, or I can get it. But it's risky.\n\nConsidering all this, maybe the best option is to discard a tile that allows me to form a chow with available tiles. Since W5 is more available, discarding W3 or W4 to get W5 is better. Let's say I choose to discard W3. Then I can keep W4 and aim for W5. Or discard W4 to keep W3 and aim for W5. Either way, getting W5 is possible.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, Play W3 is an option. So if I discard W3, I can aim for W2 or W5. Wait, if I discard W3, I can only form W1-W2-W3 if I get W2, or W3-W4-W5 if I get W5. But since I discarded W3, I can't form W3-W4-W5 unless I get W5. So maybe it's better to discard W3 and wait for W5.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W1, I can aim for W5 to form W3-W4-W5. That might be better because I already have W3 and W4. So discarding W1 allows me to keep W3 and W4, and get W5. Since W5 is available, this seems promising.\n\nSo the valid action Play W1 is an option. Let's check if that's a good move. If I discard W1, others might take it, but if I keep it, I can't form any chows with it. So discarding W1 to get W5 is better. Therefore, the best action is to play W1.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding W1 allows the formation of a chow with W3, W4, and W5 if W5 is drawn, which is available in higher quantity (4 remaining). This setup increases the chance of completing multiple chows in the Character suit.\nReason: W5 is more abundant (4 tiles) compared to other needed tiles like W2 (3) or W8 (2). Keeping W3 and W4 with W1 discarded optimizes the potential to form W3-W4-W5, a key chow for scoring patterns like All Chows or Mixed Straight.\nAnswer: Play W1"}
